#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Read-only GDDR6 memory-temperature reader for the reviewed upstream tool.

Split out of ``bc250-gddr6-temp-helper`` on purpose. This binary can only
*read*: it never calls ``unlock()``, never writes an SMU word, and contains
no code path that could. The one register it sets itself is the SMN window's
index, and only to hand back the value another client left there. That is
what lets its Polkit action use ``auth_admin_keep`` so live monitoring does
not prompt on every sample, while the patching binary keeps an uncached
``auth_admin``.

When BC250-Telemetry's own memory collector is already sampling these chips,
nothing here touches the SMU: the reader relays what that collector
published instead (see ``external_collector_lock``).

Two reads are offered, both through the reviewed ``bc250_smu`` package:

``status``
    ``smu_read`` of Queue 3's handler table is an ungated transfer-engine
    read. Comparing the entries for messages 5-7 with the payload's
    addresses tells which runtime SMU patch is active, if any, without
    touching anything.

``read``
    The same ``send_message(3, 0x6, [parity])`` primitive ``read_temp.py``
    uses: two messages, four chips each. It is refused unless ``status``
    says the patch is active: the handlers live in the *payload*, not in
    the factory firmware, so on an unpatched board those messages reach
    unknown stock handlers. Output is JSON so the desktop can draw a real
    table instead of parsing console text.

The payload Control Center 1.21.1 and earlier installed (and the one
BC250-Telemetry still bundles) answers only message 5, one chip at a time,
and waits for each chip with no time limit. It is recognised by its exact
bytes and read the old way only while BC250-Telemetry's collector is
installed, so that collector keeps working; otherwise the helper replaces
it, and until then this reports the patch as inactive.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

